  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 957 AM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nThe combination of an approaching upper-level low from the \nsoutheast, daytime heating, orographic lift, and increased moisture \nwill provide the setting for another round of afternoon/evening \nconvection today. A few storms have already developed over both the \nSacramento mountains and the Gila National Forest with the activity \nforecast to transition into the El Paso/Las Cruces area later in the \nafternoon. Localized flooding remains a concern, as max rainfall \namounts from CAMS range between 1-2 inches, but as to who will get \nhit the hardest in the lowlands is still inconclusive at this time. \nThe Gila National Forest and Sacramento Mountains will see the best \nchance for heavy rain this afternoon. Models and soundings are \nshowing downdraft CAPE values of around 1000J/kg, and are indicating \nthat thunderstorm generated outflows may produce gusty winds up to \n40 mph, which could stir up blowing dust for the lowland deserts \nthis afternoon.\n\nBy Monday, the upper-level low will be sheared off from the \nsubtropical high, but some low to mid-level moisture will remain \nover southern New Mexico. The Gila National Forest will see the best \nchance (50-60%) for heavy rain and localized flooding Monday \nafternoon, with the Sacramento Mountains coming in second (25-35%). \nSoutheastern Hudspeth County may also see some isolated \nthunderstorms Monday afternoon, while the rest of the lowlands have \na minimal chance for rain. That being said, PWAT values in the \nlowlands are still hovering around 1.2-1.3 inches for Monday, so the \nthreat of localized heavy rain cannot be ruled out.\n\nThe ridge starts to return overhead on Tuesday, drying the lowlands \nout and raising temperatures back to the triple digit mark mid- to- \nlate week. Still a chance of some relief later in the week as the \nridge's eastern flank breaks down.",
